% --- Abstract -------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
We formalize in \Lean/\Mathlib the exterior-model spinor construction for split
quadratic spaces over fields $K$ with $2\in K^\times$. The development packages
explicit hyperbolic and split-Witt presentations as first-class data carrying
the chosen model $\bigwedge W$, its chiral halves, and the transported Clifford
and spin actions. Classical endomorphism-algebra and half-spin statements are
included as infrastructure, but the paper's focus is the field-sensitive
covering theory that this chosen-model interface makes available at theorem
level.

The headline formalized results are as follows. In positive split rank, the
kernel of $\Spin(V,Q)\to\SO(V,Q)$ is $\{\pm1\}$, so the exterior-model spin
action is projective but not linear on the orthogonal image. In the hyperbolic
model we construct explicit Clifford-unit representatives for hyperbolic
transvections and chosen-line square scalings, prove their weight-$2$ torus
law, and normalize the resulting chosen-model exterior action of the torus
lift. In the same chosen model, any split Levi lift acts projectively as the
natural exterior action, and the split-line spin image is exactly the
square-scaling subgroup; moreover, this split-line map is surjective exactly
when every unit of $K$ is a square, and a nonsquare unit gives a formal
non-surjectivity theorem. For any finite basis with a distinguished line, the
split Levi image admits an exact square-determinant criterion: a Levi element
$\Lambda(g)$ lies in the spin image if and only if $\det(g)$ is a square in
$K^\times$. Equivalently, the formalized square-class character
$g\mapsto[\det(g)]\in K^\times/(K^\times)^2$ is onto, has kernel exactly the
pulled-back spin image in linear coordinates and, after transport, exactly the spin image on the
canonical split Levi subgroup. The corresponding quotients by these spin-image subgroups are
isomorphic to \(K^\times/(K^\times)^2\). These same finite-basis split-Levi
characters are also exposed under local spinor-norm-facing names, without
asserting a global orthogonal-group spinor-norm API. We also package the
Clifford-level vector-product norm formula
\(\widetilde{x}x=\prod_i(-Q(v_i))\) for \(x=\prod_i\iota(v_i)\), together with
its invertible-vector unit and square-class form, including invariance of these
product-level square classes under reordering and reversal of the chosen vector
list, cancellation of repeated invertible-vector pairs, and triviality of
duplicated products, contiguous subproducts, and separated repeated subproducts
in the square-class quotient, with wrappers for lists that first permute into
those repeated forms. The proof combines an
orthogonal factorization into transported
transvections plus one chosen-line square scaling, explicit even unitary
Clifford lifts acting by scalar-normalized exterior maps, and a top-degree
pairing normalization that proves the determinant-square necessity direction.
We also package the algebraically closed specialization: since every unit is a
square over an algebraically closed field, the split-line double-cover,
explicit chosen-model Levi-lift, and finite-basis split-Levi image corollaries
become unconditional there.
The artifact comprises
$28$ Lean source files totaling
$20{,}955$ physical lines across the repository, of which $26$ files and
$20{,}913$ physical lines lie under \code{Spinor/}, with no \texttt{sorry}.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Lean 4; Mathlib; Clifford algebras; spinor
representations; spin groups; formalized mathematics.

\noindent\textbf{MSC 2020:} 15A66; 03B35; 68V15.

% --- 1. Introduction -----------------------------------------------------

\section{Context}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Mathematical background.} Our treatment follows the classical
presentations of Lawson--Michelsohn~\cite{LawsonMichelsohn1989},
Chevalley~\cite{Chevalley1996}, Atiyah--Bott--Shapiro~\cite{AtiyahBottShapiro1964},
and Lounesto~\cite{Lounesto2001}, with the low-dimensional octonionic and
quaternionic incidences drawn from Baez~\cite{Baez2002}. The underlying
``choose a maximal isotropic and use $\bigwedge W$'' construction is classical;
the point here is to keep the choice data explicit and stable under transport.

